          }
        },
        "collapsible_item_wfLz6r": {
          "type": "collapsible_item",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "Do you offer credit terms to new customers?",
            "content": "<p>Yes, after a review of three trade references and credit references, we may grant customers credit terms (case-by-case review and approval is required).</p>",
            "custom_liquid": "",
            "page": "",
            "open": false,
            "use_subtext_color": false
          }
        },
        "collapsible_item_VcjMd4": {
          "type": "collapsible_item",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "Can I receive a sample of my label for inspection before my entire order is printed?",
            "content": "<p>Yes, we call this form of press-proof an “FAI” (First Article for Inspection), where only a handful of samples are produced for an additional fee. </p>",
            "custom_liquid": "",
            "page": "",
            "open": false,
            "use_subtext_color": false
          }
        },
        "collapsible_item_3VHFCd": {
          "type": "collapsible_item",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "Will the lead time to receive my labels be shorter for repeat orders of a pre-existing print? ",
            "content": "<p>Not necessarily. You will save some time by skipping the artwork proofing and approval phase, but your job will still move through production at our standard pace. We offer blanket order services to give customers faster turn-around on frequently ordered products.</p>",
            "custom_liquid": "",
            "page": "",
            "open": false,
            "use_subtext_color": false
          }
        },
        "collapsible_item_t7UygP": {
          "type": "collapsible_item",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "What is a blanket order? ",
            "content": "<p>A blanket order is where a customer enjoys considerable savings by ordering in large amounts, typically what they expect to use within one year, but pays for only what they receive with each release. We print and convert the entire order all at once, then store it at our climate-controlled warehouse facility until the customer is ready to use them. We can either set up recurring release dates and automatically ship or wait for our customer to call and ask for a release. With this service, the customer commits to the purchase of the entire blanket order within the time frame agreed upon.</p>",
            "custom_liquid": "",
            "page": "",
            "open": false,
            "use_subtext_color": false
          }
        },
        "collapsible_item_PzkYjq": {
          "type": "collapsible_item",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "Do you make labels for auto-apply applications? ",
            "content": "<p>Yes, labels that will be applied to bottles, cans, and other packaging in an automated packing line will be positioned on each roll according to your finished rewind code specs and will be die-cut with a measurable ‘gap’ between each label for auto-apply. </p>",
            "custom_liquid": "",
            "page": "",
            "open": false,
            "use_subtext_color": false
          }
        },
        "collapsible_item_fwHh8i": {
          "type": "collapsible_item",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "What is the ‘unwind & rewind’ direction, and do I need to know this?",
            "content": "<p>Unwind and rewind direction is important for labels that are being automatically applied by a machine, typically at a co-packer facility or bottling/canning plant. Your packaging/canning/bottling plant will work with you to determine how your labels will need to be positioned on the rolls for the best application process with their chosen equipment. Hand-applied labels do not typically require a rewind or unwind code.</p>",
            "custom_liquid": "",
            "page": "",
            "open": false,
            "use_subtext_color": false
          }
        },
        "collapsible_item_88aLVG": {
          "type": "collapsible_item",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "Why do I need to purchase printing plates for my label design? ",
            "content": "<p>If any part of your project involves flexographic printing, then a photopolymer printing plate will need to be purchased for each individual color within your design. This is a ONE-TIME charge to the customer per design.</p>",
            "custom_liquid": "",
            "page": "",
            "open": false,
            "use_subtext_color": false
          }
        },
        "collapsible_item_A96HQ3": {
          "type": "collapsible_item",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "Is there any way to avoid paying for printing plates? ",
            "content": "<p>Yes, digital printing (as opposed to flexographic printing) will not require the purchase of printing plates; however, this may not always be an option for your particular product. The printing method will be determined at the time of your order.</p>",
            "custom_liquid": "",
            "page": "",
            "open": false,
            "use_subtext_color": false
          }
        },
        "collapsible_item_xMDDf8": {
          "type": "collapsible_item",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "Is digital printing cheaper and faster than flexographic printing? ",
            "content": "<p>Sometimes. With digital printing, the customer does not have to purchase or wait for printing plates, but the per-label cost may be slightly higher than traditional flexographic printing. This is a great fit for small to medium label runs and/or clients who need labels fast with little to no up-front investment.</p><p>Flexographic printing requires the purchase of photopolymer printing plates (one for each color in the design) but rotary presses run at higher speeds, resulting in a lower per-label cost that further reduces as label volumes increase. This is a great choice for medium to large orders or for products that require other in-line applications that must be done on a rotary press. Our team of experts will assist in guiding customers to the best printing method according to their project specs, materials used, quantities needed, and any other considerations that come up.  </p>",
            "custom_liquid": "",
            "page": "",
            "open": false,
            "use_subtext_color": false
          }
        },
        "collapsible_item_wnGRm8": {
          "type": "collapsible_item",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "Can you match colors to a previously printed sample I provide? ",
            "content": "<p>Yes. We utilize the Pantone Coated Color System and mix most of our inks press-side for flexographic jobs. Changes in materials will affect colors, often dramatically, so we often send physical samples to our ink supplier along with a sample roll of the material we intend to print on for a draw-down color match. This is a more precise method that typically requires the purchase of a ‘custom mixed ink’ by the customer.  </p><p>Our digital presses utilize cutting-edge technology and software systems to calibrate color on the press and make automatic adjustments as the job is being printed should any colors wander out of the desired range.</p>",
            "custom_liquid": "",
            "page": "",
            "open": false,
            "use_subtext_color": false
          }
        }
      },
